<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<Patches>

  <!-- ====================================================================
       THE AUGMENT RACK

       Archinity.AltarFacilityExtension is fully implemented in C#
       (Source/AltarData.cs) and was carried by exactly zero defs. The altar
       already links all twelve VQE Ancients lab facilities through
       CompProperties_AffectedByFacilities, so the link lines drew, the
       facilities looked connected, and AltarModifiers.For() returned the
       identity every time. That is the "a facility declaring statFactors is
       silent everywhere" failure class in CODING_STANDARDS.md wearing a
       different hat. This file is what makes the rack mean something.

       HOW THE THREE LIVE FIELDS ARE CONSUMED

         chargeCostFactor  multiplied across active facilities, then floored
                           at 0.25 by AltarModifiers.For(). Spent twice in
                           Building_Altar: once by CanAcceptPawn as the
                           affordability gate, once by PerformRite as the
                           actual debit.
         durationFactor    multiplied across active facilities. Rite length
                           is baseHours * 2500 * durationFactor ticks.
         outcomeBonus      summed across active facilities. Success chance is
                           Clamp01(0.75 + outcomeBonus).

       INERT FIELDS, DELIBERATELY OMITTED

       AltarFacilityExtension also declares biasCategory, biasStrength and
       extraOptions. AltarModifiers.For() computes all three and no caller
       reads any of them, because the gene lottery
       (GenePoolDef.Available) has no call site. Authoring them here would
       be writing numbers that do nothing, so they are left out entirely.
       When the lottery is wired up, the prism, the redirector and the
       pathing array are the three that should get them.

       A facility that is unpowered, broken or unlinked contributes nothing:
       AltarModifiers.For() skips any facility whose CompFacility.CanBeActive
       is false. In the neolithic era, before any power exists, the whole
       rack is inert by that route alone, which is the correct behaviour.

       WHAT A FULL RACK ADDS UP TO

       "Full rack" here means one of each of the twelve, because that is what
       the campaign can actually deliver: Quest_AncientLab_Grants.xml pays out
       exactly one of each across the VQE Ancients lab chain, and the
       facilities are neverBuildable with no recipe, so the only way to hold a
       second copy of one is to complete a second site that spawns it. Several
       of them do allow more than one link (CompProperties_Facility
       maxSimultaneous is 10 on the neurostabilizer, 9 on the pump, 6 on the
       recovery array, 5 on the buffer coil, 4 on the attenuator), so the
       ceiling is higher than this in principle. It is priced for the rack you
       will realistically own.

         charge cost   0.75 * 0.85 * 0.70   = 0.446
         duration      0.70 * 0.85 * 0.85 * 1.15 * 1.10 = 0.640
         outcome       0.02 + 0.03 + 0.02 + 0.02 + 0.03 + 0.03 = +0.15
                       success 0.75 rises to 0.90

       So a complete rack roughly halves the blood price, cuts the rite to
       about two thirds of its length, and turns a 3 in 4 gamble into a 9 in
       10. It does not reach the 0.25 charge floor, and it does not reach
       certainty. The machine stays expensive and stays capable of refusing
       you, which is the whole point of it.

       TWO OF THE TWELVE ARE DELIBERATE TRADE OFFS

       The mutagen inhibitor core and the complexity harmonizer both cost
       time, matching what their own descriptions say they do: the inhibitor
       smothers a surge rather than riding it, and the harmonizer buys success
       against complexity by spending hours on it. They are the only two
       entries here that can make something worse, and both pay for it.

       ON MULTIPLAYER

       Def data only. AltarModifiers.For() walks
       LinkedFacilitiesListForReading, which is save state in a stable order,
       doing float multiplies and adds. Both clients load byte identical XML
       and read the same list, so the modifiers are identical by construction.
       No new sync surface.

       SHAPE NOTE

       Ten of the twelve ship a modExtensions node already (they carry
       VanillaQuestsExpandedAncients.ArchiteLabFacilityExtension), so those
       operations add an li into it. The archite recycler and the complexity
       harmonizer ship none, so those two insert the whole modExtensions
       element onto the ThingDef instead. Verified: the /modExtensions xpath
       matches 0 for exactly those two.
       ==================================================================== -->

  <Operation Class="PatchOperationFindMod">
    <mods>
      <li>Vanilla Quests Expanded - Ancients</li>
    </mods>
    <match Class="PatchOperationSequence">
      <operations>

        <!-- ================================================================
             OUTCOME: the buffer and stabiliser family
             ================================================================ -->

        <!-- Neurostabilizer array. "A steady hum of stabilizing neurostatic
             fields ... calm archite turbulence during injection cycles."
             The small, stackable reliability brick, and the cheapest thing
             in the rack. -->
        <!-- expect: 1 -->
        <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
          <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="VQEA_NeurostabilizerArray"]/modExtensions</xpath>
          <value>
            <li Class="Archinity.AltarFacilityExtension">
              <outcomeBonus>0.02</outcomeBonus>
            </li>
          </value>
        </li>

        <!-- Rejection buffer coil. "Suppress rejection triggering impulses in
             the nanite stream." Rejection is precisely what the altar's
             failure roll is, so this is the outcome workhorse. -->
        <!-- expect: 1 -->
        <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
          <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="VQEA_RejectionBufferCoil"]/modExtensions</xpath>
          <value>
            <li Class="Archinity.AltarFacilityExtension">
              <outcomeBonus>0.03</outcomeBonus>
            </li>
          </value>
        </li>

        <!-- Spliceframe uplink. "Scrape and decode archite fluctuation
             patterns ... maps unstable outcomes." Knowing where the stream is
             going is worth a little; it does not steer it. -->
        <!-- expect: 1 -->
        <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
          <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="VQEA_SpliceframeUplink"]/modExtensions</xpath>
          <value>
            <li Class="Archinity.AltarFacilityExtension">
              <outcomeBonus>0.02</outcomeBonus>
            </li>
          </value>
        </li>

        <!-- Trait selection prism. "Its prism array glows with shifting
             spectral patterns when a stable result is detected." Picking
             between outcomes is the dead lottery's job, so what survives here
             is the detection of a stable result. -->
        <!-- expect: 1 -->
        <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
          <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="VQEA_TraitSelectionPrism"]/modExtensions</xpath>
          <value>
            <li Class="Archinity.AltarFacilityExtension">
              <outcomeBonus>0.02</outcomeBonus>
            </li>
          </value>
        </li>

        <!-- Aberration redirector. "Reroute unstable gene echoes into
             controlled outcome channels." Rerouting the aberration rather
             than choosing among them: the strongest single outcome piece
             short of the harmonizer. -->
        <!-- expect: 1 -->
        <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
          <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="VQEA_AberrationRedirector"]/modExtensions</xpath>
          <value>
            <li Class="Archinity.AltarFacilityExtension">
              <outcomeBonus>0.03</outcomeBonus>
            </li>
          </value>
        </li>

        <!-- ================================================================
             DURATION: the infusion and recovery family
             ================================================================ -->

        <!-- Rapid infusion pump. "Push volatile nanites through the injector
             lattice at extreme speed"; its own text claims 12 hours off an
             injection. The single biggest duration cut in the rack. -->
        <!-- expect: 1 -->
        <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
          <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="VQEA_RapidInfusionPump"]/modExtensions</xpath>
          <value>
            <li Class="Archinity.AltarFacilityExtension">
              <durationFactor>0.70</durationFactor>
            </li>
          </value>
        </li>

        <!-- Cognitive recovery array. "Thought restorative pulses that soothe
             post archite neural disruption." There is no coma in the altar's
             model, so its day off the coma becomes time off the back end of
             the rite. -->
        <!-- expect: 1 -->
        <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
          <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="VQEA_CognitiveRecoveryArray"]/modExtensions</xpath>
          <value>
            <li Class="Archinity.AltarFacilityExtension">
              <durationFactor>0.85</durationFactor>
            </li>
          </value>
        </li>

        <!-- Archite pathing array. "Split the injection stream into parallel
             mutagenic pathways." Parallel pathways finish sooner. The second
             gene chance it advertises is extraOptions, which nothing reads. -->
        <!-- expect: 1 -->
        <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
          <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="VQEA_ArchitePathingArray"]/modExtensions</xpath>
          <value>
            <li Class="Archinity.AltarFacilityExtension">
              <durationFactor>0.85</durationFactor>
            </li>
          </value>
        </li>

        <!-- ================================================================
             CHARGE: the reclamation family
             ================================================================ -->

        <!-- Genomic attenuator. "Pare down excessive gene volatility during
             archite infusion." Less volatility burned means less blood
             burned. -->
        <!-- expect: 1 -->
        <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
          <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="VQEA_GenomicAttenuator"]/modExtensions</xpath>
          <value>
            <li Class="Archinity.AltarFacilityExtension">
              <chargeCostFactor>0.85</chargeCostFactor>
            </li>
          </value>
        </li>

        <!-- ================================================================
             THE TRADE OFFS
             ================================================================ -->

        <!-- Mutagen inhibitor core. "Cycle suppressive counterwaves that
             smother mutation cascades before they can form." Smothering the
             surge instead of riding it wastes almost nothing and takes
             noticeably longer. The second largest blood discount in the rack,
             paid for in hours. -->
        <!-- expect: 1 -->
        <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
          <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="VQEA_MutagenInhibitorCore"]/modExtensions</xpath>
          <value>
            <li Class="Archinity.AltarFacilityExtension">
              <chargeCostFactor>0.70</chargeCostFactor>
              <durationFactor>1.15</durationFactor>
            </li>
          </value>
        </li>

        <!-- Complexity harmonizer. "Prevents genetic complexity from reducing
             success chance, but each point of complexity will increase the
             injection procedure duration by 1 hour." Ported straight across:
             it buys success and it pays in time. -->
        <!-- expect: 1 -->
        <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
          <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="VQEA_ComplexityHarmonizer"]</xpath>
          <value>
            <modExtensions>
              <li Class="Archinity.AltarFacilityExtension">
                <outcomeBonus>0.03</outcomeBonus>
                <durationFactor>1.10</durationFactor>
              </li>
            </modExtensions>
          </value>
        </li>

        <!-- ================================================================
             CHARGE, continued: the one that ships no modExtensions node
             ================================================================ -->

        <!-- Archite recycler. "Draw unstable archite residue back into a
             containment loop ... reassembles loose nanite clusters into a
             usable capsule." It refunds the capsule upstream; here it
             reclaims the charge, which is the same idea in the altar's
             currency. Largest pure discount.

             This def ships no modExtensions node either. -->
        <!-- expect: 1 -->
        <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
          <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="VQEA_ArchiteRecycler"]</xpath>
          <value>
            <modExtensions>
              <li Class="Archinity.AltarFacilityExtension">
                <chargeCostFactor>0.75</chargeCostFactor>
              </li>
            </modExtensions>
          </value>
        </li>

      </operations>
    </match>
  </Operation>

</Patches>
